1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,790
February eighth, nineteen twelve.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:06,890
Robert Falcon Scott and his men are
walking back from the South Pole.

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:08,630
They are starving and freezing.

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:10,980
But on a rocky ridge above the
Beardmore Glacier,

5
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:12,890
they stop, to collect rocks.

6
00:00:14,137 --> 00:00:17,267
Sixteen kilos of rocks, about
thirty-five pounds.

7
00:00:17,477 --> 00:00:20,527
They drag them on the sledge for
weeks, to the very end.

8
00:00:20,837 --> 00:00:23,227
When a search party finds the
tent in November,

9
00:00:23,317 --> 00:00:25,247
the rocks are lying next to the bodies.

10
00:00:26,464 --> 00:00:30,434
Among those rocks are fossil leaves, from
a plant called Glossopteris.

11
00:00:30,624 --> 00:00:32,654
Leaves from a forest. In Antarctica.

12
00:00:33,737 --> 00:00:36,927
Today, it is the coldest and
driest place on Earth.

13
00:00:37,217 --> 00:00:40,467
But Antarctica once had forests,
rivers, and dinosaurs.

14
00:00:41,037 --> 00:00:43,537
About ninety million years ago,
there was even a swampy,

15
00:00:43,577 --> 00:00:47,027
temperate rainforest, nine hundred
kilometres from the South Pole.

16
00:00:47,277 --> 00:00:49,307
That's about five hundred and sixty miles.

17
00:00:50,025 --> 00:00:50,805
So what happened?

18
00:00:50,845 --> 00:00:52,275
How does a green continent freeze?

19
00:00:53,142 --> 00:00:56,522
The obvious answer: Antarctica used
to be near the equator,

20
00:00:56,562 --> 00:00:58,252
and then it drifted to the pole.

21
00:00:58,582 --> 00:01:02,332
But when that rainforest grew,
Antarctica was already at the pole.

22
00:01:02,422 --> 00:01:03,692
About where it is today.

23
00:01:04,885 --> 00:01:06,715
The second answer: the asteroid.

24
00:01:06,965 --> 00:01:10,455
It killed the dinosaurs, so maybe it
killed Antarctica too.

25
00:01:10,725 --> 00:01:13,715
But after the asteroid,
Antarctica got even warmer.

26
00:01:14,025 --> 00:01:15,695
Palm trees grew on its coast.

27
00:01:16,946 --> 00:01:18,036
So it's something else.

28
00:01:18,226 --> 00:01:21,316
First, the dinosaurs that lived
through months of darkness.

29
00:01:21,726 --> 00:01:23,196
Then, the slow freeze.

30
00:01:23,486 --> 00:01:27,016
And at the end, what the ice hides, and what
is starting to melt right now.

31
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:33,210
Scott's leaves were a clue.

32
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:36,230
Glossopteris fossils are found
in South America,

33
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:39,330
Africa, India, Australia, and Antarctica.

34
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:43,150
The same plant, on five continents,
separated by oceans.

35
00:01:44,378 --> 00:01:47,008
The answer is that the oceans
were not there yet.

36
00:01:47,358 --> 00:01:53,228
About three hundred million years ago, all the land on
Earth was joined in one supercontinent, Pangaea.

37
00:01:53,458 --> 00:01:56,048
Its southern half, Gondwana,
held South America,

38
00:01:56,238 --> 00:01:58,988
Africa, India, Australia, and Antarctica,

39
00:01:59,258 --> 00:02:00,008
right in the middle.

40
00:02:01,179 --> 00:02:03,449
Then Gondwana began to break apart.

41
00:02:03,919 --> 00:02:07,769
India broke away and raced north, at up
to twenty centimetres a year,

42
00:02:07,879 --> 00:02:08,909
about eight inches.

43
00:02:09,299 --> 00:02:11,469
Most plates move at two to
five centimetres,

44
00:02:11,599 --> 00:02:13,729
about as fast as your fingernails grow.

45
00:02:14,922 --> 00:02:18,612
When India hit Asia, it pushed up the
Himalayas. Remember them.

46
00:02:18,762 --> 00:02:20,692
They will matter at the end of this story.

47
00:02:21,598 --> 00:02:26,068
But Antarctica stayed behind, still joined
to South America and Australia.

48
00:02:26,978 --> 00:02:28,348
Animals could walk across it.

49
00:02:28,518 --> 00:02:32,008
And by the last age of the dinosaurs, it
was already near the pole.

50
00:02:33,224 --> 00:02:36,534
So the real question is not how
Antarctica got to the pole.

51
00:02:36,784 --> 00:02:38,614
It's how anything lived there at all.

52
00:02:41,448 --> 00:02:43,498
In the Antarctic summer of
nineteen ninety,

53
00:02:43,788 --> 00:02:48,198
a team led by geologist David Elliot was
working high on Mount Kirkpatrick,

54
00:02:48,348 --> 00:02:50,218
about four thousand metres up.

55
00:02:50,488 --> 00:02:52,138
They spotted bones in the rock.

56
00:02:53,325 --> 00:02:56,735
Paleontologist William Hammer
came to dig them out.

57
00:02:56,945 --> 00:03:01,895
His team cut about two thousand three hundred kilograms
of rock off the mountain in three weeks.

58
00:03:03,012 --> 00:03:06,802
Inside was a meat-eating dinosaur about
six and a half metres long,

59
00:03:06,892 --> 00:03:09,732
twenty-one feet, with a strange crest
running across its head,

60
00:03:09,772 --> 00:03:11,882
from side to side, like a pompadour.

61
00:03:12,372 --> 00:03:14,402
Scientists nicknamed it Elvisaurus.

62
00:03:14,672 --> 00:03:17,842
Its real name is Cryolophosaurus,
the cold-crested lizard.

63
00:03:18,172 --> 00:03:20,562
It lived about a hundred and
ninety million years ago.

64
00:03:21,065 --> 00:03:24,575
Nearby was a long-necked
plant-eater, Glacialisaurus.

65
00:03:24,725 --> 00:03:28,515
Two very different dinosaurs, on one
mountain that now stands in ice.

66
00:03:29,526 --> 00:03:33,196
Later in the age of dinosaurs,
Antarctica was richer still.

67
00:03:33,606 --> 00:03:38,436
In twenty seventeen, a German research ship drilled
into the seabed off West Antarctica.

68
00:03:38,886 --> 00:03:41,946
The core held soil, and a
three-metre web of fossil roots,

69
00:03:41,986 --> 00:03:45,416
from a swampy rainforest that grew
ninety million years ago.

70
00:03:46,399 --> 00:03:49,649
In twenty twenty-four, the same core
gave up something else:

71
00:03:49,819 --> 00:03:52,129
the first amber ever found in Antarctica.

72
00:03:52,879 --> 00:03:55,109
Drops of resin from the trees
of that forest.

73
00:03:56,309 --> 00:03:59,559
But that forest was nine hundred
kilometres from the pole.

74
00:03:59,909 --> 00:04:02,319
Every winter, the sun
disappeared for months.

75
00:04:02,589 --> 00:04:04,599
So how did anything survive the dark?

76
00:04:05,729 --> 00:04:09,139
In nineteen eighty-six, two
Argentine geologists,

77
00:04:09,189 --> 00:04:14,739
Eduardo Olivero and Roberto Scasso, found an
armoured dinosaur on James Ross Island.

78
00:04:15,149 --> 00:04:17,139
Antarctopelta, the Antarctic shield.

79
00:04:17,529 --> 00:04:21,679
For forty years, it was known as the first
dinosaur ever found in Antarctica.

80
00:04:22,625 --> 00:04:26,965
Then, in twenty twenty-six, scientists
described a bone that had sat in a British

81
00:04:27,005 --> 00:04:32,185
Antarctic Survey drawer for forty years: the
tail bone of a long-necked titanosaur,

82
00:04:32,225 --> 00:04:36,035
collected by geologist Michael Thomson in
December nineteen eighty-five,

83
00:04:36,445 --> 00:04:37,295
one month earlier.

84
00:04:38,452 --> 00:04:42,852
On James Ross Island and its neighbours lived
small two-legged plant-eaters,

85
00:04:42,892 --> 00:04:45,802
a raptor-like hunter, and,
from a single tooth,

86
00:04:45,892 --> 00:04:47,162
a duck-billed dinosaur.

87
00:04:48,417 --> 00:04:50,727
In twenty twenty-one,
scientists in Alaska,

88
00:04:50,797 --> 00:04:53,277
which was also inside the polar
circle back then,

89
00:04:53,317 --> 00:04:56,087
found the bones of baby
dinosaurs, just hatched.

90
00:04:56,437 --> 00:04:58,767
A hatchling cannot walk
thousands of kilometres.

91
00:04:58,957 --> 00:05:01,007
So their parents stayed through
the polar night.

92
00:05:01,357 --> 00:05:04,127
Scientists think the same was
probably true in the south.

93
00:05:04,772 --> 00:05:08,232
In the seas swam giant sea lizards,
long-necked reptiles,

94
00:05:08,272 --> 00:05:10,552
and ammonites, shelled relatives of squid,

95
00:05:10,592 --> 00:05:11,502
by the thousands.

96
00:05:12,598 --> 00:05:17,028
Then, sixty-six million years ago, an
asteroid about ten kilometres wide,

97
00:05:17,258 --> 00:05:18,728
six miles, hit Mexico.

98
00:05:21,809 --> 00:05:25,319
On Seymour Island, the rock layers
record what happened next.

99
00:05:25,709 --> 00:05:28,899
Scientists counted more than six
thousand fossils there.

100
00:05:29,129 --> 00:05:31,759
Around two thirds of the species
vanished, suddenly.

101
00:05:32,149 --> 00:05:35,599
The last seven kinds of ammonite
disappeared in a single pulse.

102
00:05:36,696 --> 00:05:38,046
The dinosaurs were gone.

103
00:05:38,176 --> 00:05:39,966
But Antarctica itself survived.

104
00:05:40,136 --> 00:05:43,286
And birds are dinosaurs too, and
their line made it through.

105
00:05:43,696 --> 00:05:47,086
On an Antarctic island, scientists
found the skull of Vegavis,

106
00:05:47,196 --> 00:05:50,586
a cousin of today's ducks that lived
alongside the last dinosaurs.

107
00:05:50,976 --> 00:05:52,646
It was described in twenty twenty-five.

108
00:05:53,885 --> 00:05:55,465
And then the strangest chapter began.

109
00:05:55,505 --> 00:05:57,115
Antarctica got warmer.

110
00:05:57,918 --> 00:06:02,138
In twenty ten, the drillship JOIDES Resolution
drilled into the seabed off East

111
00:06:02,178 --> 00:06:05,128
Antarctica, under four
kilometres of water.

112
00:06:05,538 --> 00:06:09,428
The expedition was led by Carlota
Escutia and Henk Brinkhuis.

113
00:06:10,057 --> 00:06:14,077
In mud fifty-three million years old, they
found pollen from palm trees,

114
00:06:14,117 --> 00:06:16,307
and from relatives of the baobab.

115
00:06:16,637 --> 00:06:20,107
On the coast of Antarctica, there was a
near-tropical rainforest.

116
00:06:21,313 --> 00:06:23,983
Even in winter, in the darkness
of the polar night,

117
00:06:24,073 --> 00:06:27,903
the coast stayed warmer than ten degrees
Celsius, fifty Fahrenheit.

118
00:06:28,967 --> 00:06:30,897
And Antarctica was not empty.

119
00:06:31,347 --> 00:06:35,827
In nineteen eighty-two, Michael Woodburne and
William Zinsmeister found a tiny jaw

120
00:06:35,867 --> 00:06:37,197
on Seymour Island.

121
00:06:37,487 --> 00:06:40,177
It belonged to a marsupial, a
pouched mammal,

122
00:06:40,287 --> 00:06:42,737
the first land mammal ever
found in Antarctica.

123
00:06:44,195 --> 00:06:47,635
Scientists think marsupials used
Antarctica as a bridge,

124
00:06:47,675 --> 00:06:50,525
walking from South America to Australia.

125
00:06:50,975 --> 00:06:54,035
Australia's marsupials, from
koalas to kangaroos,

126
00:06:54,075 --> 00:06:55,745
probably arrived this way.

127
00:06:56,837 --> 00:06:59,067
But this was Antarctica's last summer.

128
00:06:59,277 --> 00:07:01,107
And it ended without a single disaster.

129
00:07:04,122 --> 00:07:05,932
The first change was in the air.

130
00:07:06,142 --> 00:07:09,212
Over millions of years, carbon
dioxide began to fall.

131
00:07:10,075 --> 00:07:12,845
One leading idea brings back
the Himalayas.

132
00:07:13,095 --> 00:07:16,785
As they rose, rain wore down huge
areas of fresh rock.

133
00:07:17,035 --> 00:07:20,985
That weathering pulls carbon dioxide out of
the air and locks it into stone.

134
00:07:22,216 --> 00:07:24,146
The second change was in the ocean.

135
00:07:24,876 --> 00:07:28,176
Australia pulled away, and a deep seaway
opened south of Tasmania,

136
00:07:28,216 --> 00:07:31,016
about thirty-five and a half
million years ago.

137
00:07:31,056 --> 00:07:33,786
And at some point, South
America let go too.

138
00:07:34,016 --> 00:07:36,986
The Drake Passage opened, eight
hundred kilometres of sea.

139
00:07:38,107 --> 00:07:40,977
Now water could flow all the way
around Antarctica.

140
00:07:41,427 --> 00:07:46,677
Today, that current moves about a hundred and seventy
million cubic metres of water every second.

141
00:07:46,927 --> 00:07:50,197
That's more than a hundred times all the
rivers on Earth combined.

142
00:07:50,487 --> 00:07:53,077
It acts like a wall, keeping
warm water away.

143
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:56,290
But scientists now think the
wall was a helper,

144
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:57,150
not the cause.

145
00:07:57,660 --> 00:08:00,060
In two thousand three, two
climate modellers,

146
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:03,090
Robert DeConto and David
Pollard, found the switch:

147
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:06,690
below a certain level of carbon
dioxide, ice could stay.

148
00:08:07,881 --> 00:08:10,551
About thirty-four million
years ago, it did.

149
00:08:10,781 --> 00:08:14,451
In just a few hundred thousand years, an ice
sheet spread across the continent.

150
00:08:15,624 --> 00:08:17,014
And ice feeds itself.

151
00:08:17,264 --> 00:08:20,014
Fresh snow reflects up to ninety
percent of sunlight.

152
00:08:20,344 --> 00:08:22,294
A forest reflects maybe a fifth.

153
00:08:22,564 --> 00:08:25,634
More ice means less heat, and less
heat means more ice.

154
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:28,650
The forests shrank to the coasts.

155
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:31,330
One of the last trees was the
southern beech.

156
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:36,050
Its fossil leaves have been found about five
hundred kilometres from the South Pole.

157
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:39,150
Its relatives still grow
today, in Patagonia,

158
00:08:39,260 --> 00:08:40,610
Tasmania and New Zealand.

159
00:08:41,822 --> 00:08:47,092
In the Dry Valleys, the last mosses and insects died
out about fourteen million years ago.

160
00:08:47,362 --> 00:08:50,332
A few dwarf beech shrubs may have
hung on even later.

161
00:08:50,462 --> 00:08:52,572
But the continent now belonged to the ice.

162
00:08:53,753 --> 00:08:55,603
But the ice did not erase that world.

163
00:08:55,693 --> 00:08:56,723
It only covered it.

164
00:08:59,339 --> 00:09:03,539
In nineteen fifty-eight, Soviet scientists
sent sound waves into the ice,

165
00:09:03,579 --> 00:09:05,449
in the middle of East Antarctica.

166
00:09:05,899 --> 00:09:06,929
They found mountains.

167
00:09:08,013 --> 00:09:11,623
Fifty years later, two small planes
flew over them with radar.

168
00:09:12,173 --> 00:09:16,293
Geophysicist Fausto Ferraccioli said they
were the size of the European Alps,

169
00:09:16,333 --> 00:09:18,343
with sharp peaks and deep valleys.

170
00:09:18,773 --> 00:09:20,043
The Gamburtsev Mountains.

171
00:09:20,293 --> 00:09:21,963
No human has ever seen them.

172
00:09:23,075 --> 00:09:25,685
Their roots formed about a
billion years ago.

173
00:09:25,915 --> 00:09:29,545
Some scientists think the ice sheet was
born on these very peaks,

174
00:09:29,715 --> 00:09:31,385
and it has protected them ever since.

175
00:09:32,517 --> 00:09:34,087
There are deeper places too.

176
00:09:34,277 --> 00:09:39,007
Under Denman Glacier, the rock lies more than three
and a half kilometres below sea level.

177
00:09:39,217 --> 00:09:41,907
It is the deepest point on land
anywhere on Earth.

178
00:09:43,108 --> 00:09:44,178
And there is water.

179
00:09:44,268 --> 00:09:46,758
Hundreds of lakes lie under
the Antarctic ice.

180
00:09:47,188 --> 00:09:51,258
The biggest, Lake Vostok, is about two
hundred and fifty kilometres long.

181
00:09:52,342 --> 00:09:54,092
The ice itself is a record.

182
00:09:54,502 --> 00:09:58,682
In January twenty twenty-five, a European team
drilled down two thousand eight

183
00:09:58,722 --> 00:10:03,012
hundred metres and reached ice at least one
point two million years old.

184
00:10:03,242 --> 00:10:05,712
Every layer holds bubbles of ancient air.

185
00:10:08,796 --> 00:10:10,526
So, in the end, what froze Antarctica?

186
00:10:11,355 --> 00:10:13,325
Not because it drifted to the pole.

187
00:10:13,495 --> 00:10:15,285
It was already there when it was green.

188
00:10:15,495 --> 00:10:16,905
Not because of the asteroid.

189
00:10:17,155 --> 00:10:18,625
It got warmer after that.

190
00:10:19,569 --> 00:10:22,649
It froze because carbon
dioxide fell, slowly,

191
00:10:22,689 --> 00:10:24,129
over millions of years.

192
00:10:24,169 --> 00:10:26,649
And when the ocean closed a
ring around it,

193
00:10:26,689 --> 00:10:27,639
the ice could stay.

194
00:10:28,694 --> 00:10:34,104
Today, carbon dioxide is rising again, past four
hundred and twenty parts per million.

195
00:10:34,234 --> 00:10:37,744
And at the edge of West Antarctica,
the ice is already moving.

196
00:10:38,174 --> 00:10:40,764
The Thwaites Glacier is about
the size of Florida.

197
00:10:41,054 --> 00:10:43,564
Its front is a hundred and
twenty kilometres wide.

198
00:10:44,183 --> 00:10:49,013
The ice flowing out of it and its neighbours has more
than doubled since the nineteen nineties.

199
00:10:49,403 --> 00:10:52,353
Tides push warm seawater
kilometres under the ice,

200
00:10:52,403 --> 00:10:53,573
melting it from below.

201
00:10:54,757 --> 00:10:58,527
If Thwaites collapsed completely, sea
levels would rise about sixty-five

202
00:10:58,567 --> 00:11:00,507
centimetres, about two feet.

203
00:11:00,857 --> 00:11:03,507
All of West Antarctica holds more
than three metres,

204
00:11:03,597 --> 00:11:04,507
over ten feet.

205
00:11:04,997 --> 00:11:08,597
Scientists say a full collapse is
unlikely in the next few decades,

206
00:11:08,637 --> 00:11:10,267
but the retreat is speeding up.

207
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:13,320
That would not bring back the rainforest.

208
00:11:13,510 --> 00:11:15,860
But it would redraw every
coastline on the map.

209
00:11:16,641 --> 00:11:19,021
If you could walk through
Antarctica for one day,

210
00:11:19,061 --> 00:11:23,641
in the age of Cryolophosaurus, or among the
palms fifty million years ago,

211
00:11:23,681 --> 00:11:24,531
which would you choose?

212
00:11:24,901 --> 00:11:25,891
Tell me in the comments.

213
00:11:26,401 --> 00:11:28,651
And tell me which place we
should explain next.

214
00:11:29,744 --> 00:11:33,474
Scott's rocks are in London today, at
the Natural History Museum.

215
00:11:33,864 --> 00:11:36,374
Sixteen kilos they refused
to leave behind.

216
00:11:36,724 --> 00:11:39,474
Two years later, those leaves were
described in London,

217
00:11:39,644 --> 00:11:42,914
one more clue that the coldest place
on Earth was once green.

218
00:11:43,284 --> 00:11:44,454
This was How It's Run.
